503.14. Mountain Springs zoning district.
A. Permitted Uses.
1. Within the Mountain Springs Zoning District, each parcel will be designated as one of the following five (5) subareas with permitted uses restricted by subarea as indicated below. Upon approval of a final subdivision plat for the development of any portion of the property within the Mountain Springs Zoning District, each parcel on the newly created plat shall be designated subject to the limitations below by the subdivision applicant as one of the following five subareas: MS-MUV (Mountain Springs - Mixed Use Village); MS-RR (Mountain Springs - Rural Retreat); MS-HR (Mountain Springs - Hamlet Residential); MS-RE (Mountain Springs-Residential Estate); or MS-MF (Mountain Springs - Mini-Farm) and these subarea designations shall be added to the records of the City of Manchester's official Zoning Index. Changes in the designation of a parcel from one subarea to another shall be allowed by approval of the Planning Commission or by subsequent further subdivision and the recording of a new plat.
Limitations on the designation of Subareas: The Mountain Springs zoning district is designed to: allow for a mix of uses; allow for flexibility in the location of those uses; protect the rural character of the district; protect the natural beauty of the district; and allow for future flexibility in site planning as the area is developed over a number of years. In order to accomplish all of these goals, the following regulations shall apply:
a. The maximum percentage of the Mountain Springs zoning district that can be designated as MS-MUV (Mountain Springs - Mixed Use Village) shall be twenty percent (20%).
b. The maximum percentage of impervious surface allowed across the entire zoning district shall be thirty-five percent (35%).
c. A minimum of forty percent 40% of the Mountain Springs zoning district must remain as green space. Calculations for this green space shall include both undisturbed areas held in common ownership by a homeowners association and undisturbed areas on privately-owned parcels.
d. The maximum number of dwelling units across the entire Mountain Springs zoning district shall be seven hundred and eighty (780) units.
e. The MS-RE (Mountain Springs-Residential Estate) subarea is designated to protect the forested nature of the subarea while also allowing for the construction of new dwelling units. In order to accomplish this, there should be minimum disruption to the land during construction. Thus, mass clearing and grading across multiple residential lots is not permitted in this subarea, and each parcel shall include a minimum twenty-five foot undisturbed buffer next to all adjacent parcels.
f. In order to minimize any potential nuisances associated with hobby farming activities, areas designated as MS-MF (Mountain Springs - Mini-Farm) should be grouped together and must include a fifty-foot undisturbed buffer between a property designated as MS-MF (Mountain Springs - Mini-Farm) and adjacent properties designated as MS-HR (Mountain Springs - Hamlet Residential) or MS-RE (Mountain Spring - Residential Estate).
2. Permitted uses shall be indicated in the below Permitted Uses chart as indicated by "P".
3. Uses allowable only by the granting of a special use permit are indicated by "SUP". Criteria for granting special use permits shall be:
a. Compatibility to neighborhood
b. Minimum traffic impact
c. Adequacy of parking
4. Uses not indicated as being allowed in the below Permitted Uses chart shall be deemed to be prohibited.
5. Uses listed as permitted in the Mountain Springs zoning district that involve the manufacture, purchase, or retail sale of alcoholic beverages for consumption on or off premises are only permitted if also allowed by and meeting all requirements in the city's code Chapter 6 - Alcoholic Beverages.
